#N canvas 100 100 560 600 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win 30;
#X msg 40 100 create;
#X msg 130 100 destroy;
#X msg 40 150 dimen 800 500;
#X msg 40 180 dimen 400 300;
#X msg 40 220 offset 200 200;
#X msg 40 250 title my_custom_title;
#X msg 40 290 border 0;
#X msg 130 290 border 1;
#X msg 40 320 fullscreen 1;
#X msg 130 320 fullscreen 0;
#X msg 40 360 profile 1;
#X msg 130 360 profile 0;
#X msg 40 390 fps;
#X floatatom 130 390 0 0 0 0 fps - -;
#X text 250 150 dimen/offset/title/border/fullscreen \, ported from
Gem's gemwin. the creation arg (30 here) sets the target frame rate
\, exactly like [gemwin 30] - it's stored as a tick interval (1000/fps
ms) instead of the old fixed 16ms. profile 1 turns on per-frame timing
(like Gem \, off by default to avoid overhead) \; fps then reports
the last frame's instantaneous rate through the left outlet - should
read close to 30 now.;
#X msg 250 320 display 1;
#X msg 250 350 display 2;
#X msg 250 380 displays;
#X text 40 420 Fullscreen is a borderless window covering one display - instant\ \, with no macOS fullscreen Space and no animation\ \, so several windows can sit fullscreen on several displays. ESC leaves it (also when the keyboard is in a Pd window instead of the jet window - the object listens to the same key broadcast as [key]\, and with several fullscreen windows ESC leaves all of them) \; "fullscreen 0" does too. "fullscreen 1" may be sent before "create". "display N" chooses the display it covers (1 = primary\ \, 2 = next ...\ \; 0 = the one the window is on)\ \, "displays" prints all displays with their size and offset. "offset x y" uses Pd screen coordinates: origin at the top left of the primary display\ \, y down\ \, a display to the right of a 1920 wide primary starts at x = 1920. dimen/offset/border sent while fullscreen take effect when it ends.;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 0 0;
#X connect 4 0 0 0;
#X connect 5 0 0 0;
#X connect 6 0 0 0;
#X connect 7 0 0 0;
#X connect 8 0 0 0;
#X connect 9 0 0 0;
#X connect 10 0 0 0;
#X connect 11 0 0 0;
#X connect 12 0 0 0;
#X connect 13 0 0 0;
#X connect 0 0 14 0;
#X connect 16 0 0 0;
#X connect 17 0 0 0;
#X connect 18 0 0 0;
